    "long_instructions": "## Do This:\r\n\r\nExplore the code for this app. After each change, interact with the app to see how it impacted its functionality.\r\n\r\n1. **Change a starting value.** Find where the program sets the starting value for a variable. Change one of these values (for example: change `health = 20` to a different number, or change the `username`).\r\n\r\n2. **Change what the button does.** Find where the button changes the value of the health variable. Change how much it changes (for example: `change + 5` to `+ 10` or `- 1`).\r\n\r\n3. **Use text instead of a number.** Change the health variable's starting value to text instead of a number (for example: `let health = \"full\"`). What happens when you click the button?\r\n\r\n4. **Prevent a value from changing.** Change `let health` to `const health`. What happens when you click the button?\r\n\r\n5. **(Optional) Challenge.** Find the variable that controls the avatar image. Change the value to a different image in the `images` folder.",
    "require_edit_to_continue": "false",
    "teacher_markdown": " **Students may think changing a variable automatically updates the page**\r\n\r\nSome students may expect the displayed health, username, or rank to change immediately when they edit the variable. Encourage them to test the app and observe what updates on screen by asking: **\"Where does the code take the variable's value and display it on the page?\"** This helps students connect variables to DOM updates.\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n- `let health = 20;`\r\n- `document.getElementById(\"healthDisplay\").textContent = health;`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may be surprised when text behaves differently than numbers**\r\n\r\nWhen students change `health` from a number to text, they may expect the button to keep adding health normally. Ask: **\"What do you think happens when JavaScript combines text and a number?\"** Encourage students to make a prediction before testing and use the result to explore data types.\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n- `let health = \"full\";`\r\n- `health = health + 5;`\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may not understand why `const` causes an error**\r\n\r\nSome students may think `let` and `const` are interchangeable. When the app stops working after changing `let health` to `const health`, ask: **\"What is the program trying to do to the variable when the button is clicked?\"** Help students recognize that `const` values cannot be reassigned after they are created.\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n- `const health = 20;`\r\n- `health = health + 5;`",
